1 NAME Abraham /Koosis/
2 GIVN Abraham
2 SURN Koosis
1 SEX M
1 BURI
2 PLAC San Rafael, Marin County, California, United States of America
2 ADDR
1 BIRT
2 DATE 27 MAR 1903
2 PLAC Proskurov, Khmelnystskyi, Ukraine
2 ADDR
1 DEAT
2 DATE 9 JAN 1997
2 PLAC Alameda County, California, United States of America
2 ADDR
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} === GEDCOM Note ===
2 CONT Autobio material from: Child of Was and Revolution: Memoirs of Abe Koosis
2 CONT
2 CONT I don't remember how this happened, but I found out that Abe Koosis wrote a memoir and had 500 copies made.  I bought one and it arrived Saturday.
2 CONT
2 CONT It's incredibly frustrating because he refers to his parents, grandparents, mother's grandparents - but never mentions their names.  He names his sisters.  There's a picture of 5 of them in the US with "Mother" in 1925.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was born ''in Russia" March 27, 1904.  Apparently they lived in Proskuroff where his father's family lived. He died very young of pneumonia when Abe was 3 (1907.)
2 CONT
2 CONT His Mother moved back to her birthplace, Ostropol, with 6 children. He thinks she was about 30. Sura Pesse emigrated 12/1908.  (My father was born between 1899 and 1902 depending on which source you believe.  I believe Jan 4, 1901
2 CONC  - using the age on the boat. )   I guess it'ss possible that sisters and cousins visited each other and  his sisters knew my aunts.
2 CONT
2 CONT At 7, (1911) he went back to Proskuroff (under the supervision of his 11 year old sister) to go to a school and stay with his father's brother or his father's parents.The grandparents were cold people and prayed a lot. But he enjo
2 CONC yed his cousins.   I don't know how his sister got home.
2 CONT
2 CONT They decided he was too young and sent him back.
2 CONT
2 CONT His Ostropol family was his mother, 5 sisters, and Maternal grandfather,Yaakov / Jukel.  The sisters were Rachel, Sarah, Adele, Sonia, and Ethel.  Grandmother died fairly young and none of the grandchildren got to know her.  He he
2 CONC ard she had wanted to marry somone else, but her father insisted she marry his grandfather because he was well educated and could be an asset to their business.  He heard she was very beautiful but unhappy.
2 CONT
2 CONT His maternal grandfather then decided to give up all his possessions.  He gave a house to Abe's mother and then moved in with them.  He became a settler of disputes in Jewish Law and advisor to the Jewish community. He would write
2 CONC  letters and petitions on their behalf.  The oldest sister (15) because he opened the door to her room when her boyfriend was in there and told the boy he should visit other girls. Stores were closed and there was a big procession
2 CONC  when he died.
2 CONT
2 CONT He mentions 2 big cousins who were sons of his grandfather's brother
2 CONT (his great uncle)  They would be his mother's 1st cousins and his 1st cousin once removed.  That great-Uncle, Chaim Leib, was a black sheep, going into distilling rather than studying.  He and his wife had a large family and he wo
2 CONC uld mix up their name, e.g., You are Yossel?  No Motel?    So Yossel and Moteel would have been the same generation / 1st cousins to Mindel, Sophie, Scholom, etc.)
2 CONT
2 CONT The sons came to America and then brought both parents.
2 CONT
2 CONT His 15 yr old oldest sister Rachel, (named in a 1913 picture caption.) got involved with revolutionary activities - then took off to America when she was about 16 shortly before WWI broke out August 1914.
2 CONT
2 CONT Abe was old enoough to go back and continue his studies while going home for summers.  his story continues, intertwined with experiences of the war, politics, anti-Semitism and pogroms.
2 CONT
2 CONT Abe mentions a miracle in the form of an American making arrangements to take a group to America,  There were several sources of money, including his sister who had left 7 years earllier.  They could take 2 of Abe's sisters, but t
2 CONC hey got him to include Abe.  So this must be 1920. but it was a long journey with several long waits. They sailed from Liverpool on the Imperator.  They found his sister and an uncle waiting when they were released. Abe stayed wit
2 CONC h a cousin on the Lower East ide. His sisters stayed with other relatives.
2 CONT
2 CONT His fathers parents were aged and poor, his mother's were dead.  At that point, 2 sisters and his mother were left.  They did emigrate before the 1925 picture after the mother and sisters reunited in New York while Abe was in Cali
2 CONC fornia..
2 CONT
2 CONT He lived in NY 1921-25. Then an uncle visited from LA (commercially successful)  He went to UCLA. Met Ella. She went to Switzerland to study dance. Married Frieda Levine. 1929.   Turmoil in politics and being labeled subversive. E
2 CONC ventually divorced.  Meanwhile saw Ella Serrurier again.  Eventually married and had sons Don and Tom.  Ella dec 1977.  Abe was still living at the end of the book
2 CONT
2 CONT I sure wish he had identified the names of the cousins, uncles, and other relatives he referred to in his narrative.
2 CONT
2 CONT NOTE ON NAMING PATTERNS
2 CONT
2 CONT The names of the sisters reflect the names of two of Mindel's sisters and her sister Sura Pesse's children's names.
2 CONT
2 CONT Sarah was most likely named after the same person as my graandmother and Sonia was likely named after the same person as Sophie/Sprintza.
2 CONT Rachel: My grandmother had a daughter named Ruchel who became Rose
2 CONT Adele: My grandmother had a daughter named Udje who became Anna
2 CONT Ethel:  My grandmother had a daughter named Esther.
