1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Origin of his name is interesting. One of the original shareholders in the Africa theatre on Riebeeck Square (on Bree St) was Haynes Wade Battersby, an Anglo-Irish gentleman at the Cape for some years. 
2 CONT
2 CONT William Haynes Wade Barends’s mother, Jacobina Petronella Barends (Dianas) possibly a freed slave from Bengal, gave birth to a child named Haynes Wade Battersby before she began her relationship with Gotlieb Barends. One has to as
2 CONC sume that Haynes Wade Battersby (senior) was the father. Gotlieb’s sister married a van Driel and the van Driel’s employed Haynes Wade Battersby (Jnr) in their business and in turn named grandchildren after Haynes Wade Battersby. 
2 CONC The Van Driel General Store was in the basement of the theatre (later converted into St Stephens Church) which his namesake part owned and William in due course took it over and passed it to his son Samuel. 
2 CONT
2 CONT William was christened along with two sisters in 1831 as a ten year old by the Rev. Barnabus Shaw in the Wesleyan chapel - the earliest Methodist church in Cape Town. He later joined the NG church.
2 CONT
2 CONT Charles Samuel was born to William's wife 3 years before their marriage and initially baptised Charles Samuel Serrurier. Charles was treated as son of William and took over his business. He was buried  in same cemetery alongside h
2 CONC is parents. There remains a question as to whether he was Williams biological son, or whether formally adopted. 
