1 NOTE {geni:about_me} === GEDCOM Note ===
2 CONT Lena married Max Greengarten.  I don't have any memories of him, probably because he died before I was born.  In the 1930 census Aunt Lena was head of house with their 3 daughters living there.  I have a picture of her with a gran
2 CONC dchild, Michael - who may have been named for Max.   I remember her as having white hair at events.  My father sent a check every month made out to her or to a home in the 1940's. I thought she lived in an institution, but my rese
2 CONC arch shows that she was the contact person for brother Abram Silverman,  who lived in a Convalescent Center in Newark.  She also declared intent to naturalize and was naturalized in the appropriate time frame.  Finally there are  
2 CONC family pictures where she is with her siblings, probably into the 1960s
2 CONT
2 CONT The city directories show they lived in Newark and in the 1920 census, they already had 3 daughters.  The oldest, Esther (I knew her as Esta from staying with Uncle Yalek's daughter Fagel Levinson) was 8 in that census.  Max was a
2 CONC  painter.  He appeared in the directories through 1924.  Then in 1935 Lena appeared as the widow of Max.  She lived with the two younger girls and Esther lived separately and was a bookkeeper.
2 CONT
2 CONT The census also shows Lena and her parents' birthplace as Galitzia, which is not reflected in records of any other Silvermans.  Max and his parents' birthplaces are Galitzia.  Possibly the census takers write the husband's informa
2 CONC tion for the wife.  I did not come across any other information where Ostropol was considered part of Galitzia.
2 CONT
2 CONT Lena submitted INTENT for naturalization in 1941 (Good for her!)  and naturalized in 1944 - so any stories of being in a home at that time were incorrect.  Her witnesses were Susie Seidel and Sarah Steinberg, housewives living on 
2 CONC Belmont and Watson Streets - which various family members have lived on.  Picture is included.
2 CONT
2 CONT Her daughter Esther, Esta, never married and was very close to Uncle Yalek's daughter Fagel.  I used to see her at their house on holidays.  I don't know why I never heard of Sophie.  I thought she  must have died young, but there
2 CONC  is a social security document that shows otherwise.   There is a newspaper announcement of Sylvia's marriage to Irving Fruchter, so that must be the grandchild.  She seems to have also died fairly young.  I don't know what happen
2 CONC ed to the child.  However, I have heard the name Fruchter and there is a picture with a young man and teenager in it and one of the Johnstown cousins thought it might be Sylvia's husband and son.   (Note: It could also be Max Silv
2 CONC erman's son-in-law Henry Shenkel with his and his daughter's son David. Helen died when David was a young teen.)
