1 NOTE {geni:about_me} === GEDCOM Note ===
2 CONT In the 1910 census, the family used the name Silberman.  The name Louis was used for a son who was 9 and for a son who was 14.  Yalek/Alex was not listed.  There was probably a transcription error and Louis was the younger son and
2 CONC  Yalek was 14.
2 CONT
2 CONT Pat Silverman's childhood recollections: I heard many stories about Uncle Louie and on balance, it didn't seem like he found a reliable occupation.  When I was young and vacationed with family in Atlantic City, he was selling ice 
2 CONC cream on the beach.  I also heard he ran for mayor of Atlantic City.  I also heard he was in the army in WWI and took a train far away from the site of deployment the day before his unit was shipping out and then called his comman
2 CONC ding officer that he couldn't get back in time.  When we were in Atlantic City, he said he could get us in to a popular restaurant with a long line.  He took us to the back door and through the kitchen, but we were seated.   Woody
2 CONC  stayed with us for a while when I was very young.  I heard  Uncle Louie could not maintain their home and Aunt Bessie went to her sister's home and Woody came to our home.  I don't know why - maybe finances, maybe discord.  .  Wo
2 CONC ody was about 15 or 16.  I think  my mother wanted him to live with us, but my father said he belonged with his parents when t hey were able to take him back.    Uncle Louis came to our house once in a cab from the train station a
2 CONC nd borrowed money.  My mother wanted him to stay, but he kept the cab waiting and left.  This is a vague recollection.  This probably says as much about the relationship between my father and Uncle Louie (and maybe the brothers an
2 CONC d sisters) as it says about Uncle Louie. I think they knew they could depend on each other, but I also think my father was the most finacially stable one.   When I was young and went to the family events, Uncle Louie was usually t
2 CONC here.
2 CONT
2 CONT Uncle Louis married Bessie and their only child was Woody.  I think his name was Woodrow Wilson.  I have some details in an email from Woody's wife, Carol.  Woody's Bar Mitzvah picture deom 1946 says Woodrow Silverman.  Later, Unc
2 CONC le Louis changed their family name to Kodak - I heard the story that he wanted to have the name of the family in the "old country"  Who knows how it might have been spelled.  I have seen Chodak, Chukid, Kukik and many variations o
2 CONC n family lists on Jewish Genealogy.  Apparently no one in Atlantic City knew Uncle Louis by that name, so he changed his name and Bessie's back to Silverman and left Woody with Kodak.  My father didn't like the name change.  Woody
2 CONC  must have been 14 when that happened, so I don't think he had a choice.
2 CONT
2 CONT Legal name change from Silverman to "Kodek" September 30 1946  DCR File 11324 shows Sept. 03, 1946 for Louis, Bessie and Woodrow.  There is no record of a change back for Louis and Bessie, although they started using Silverman aga
2 CONC in.  https://wwwnet1.state.nj.us/DOS/Admin/ArchivesDBPortal/NameChanges.aspx
