2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT Sir Alexander James Beresford Beresford Hope (born Alexander James Beresford Hope) was a British author and Conservative politician who served in the House of Commons for several decades. He was born on January 25, 1820, in London
2 CONC , the third and youngest son of Thomas Hope, a merchant and art patron, and the Honorable Louisa Beresford. His family was of Scottish descent but had established a significant banking business in the Netherlands before returning 
2 CONC to Britain. He was educated at Harrow School and Trinity College, Cambridge, where he graduated in 1841 and was a founding member of the Cambridge Camden Society. In 1854, he assumed the additional surname of Beresford by royal li
2 CONC cense upon inheriting the estates of his stepfather, William Carr Beresford, 1st Viscount Beresford.
2 CONT
2 CONT On July 7, 1842, Beresford Hope married Lady Mildred Arabella Charlotte Henrietta Gascoyne-Cecil at Hatfield House, Hertfordshire. Lady Mildred was the eldest daughter of James Brownlow William Gascoyne-Cecil, 2nd Marquess of Sali
2 CONC sbury, and the sister of the future Prime Minister Robert Gascoyne-Cecil. The couple had ten children, including three sons and seven daughters; notable among them were Philip Beresford-Hope, Bridget Beresford-Hope, and Mary Franc
2 CONC es Beresford-Hope. His wife, a prominent figure in London society, predeceased him, dying in Nice on March 18, 1881. The family resided primarily at Bedgebury Park in Kent and Beresford Hall in Staffordshire, maintaining a lineage
2 CONC  connected to several influential political and noble families.
2 CONT
2 CONT Beresford Hope's political career began in 1841 when he was elected as the Member of Parliament for Maidstone, a seat he held until 1852 and again from 1857 to 1859. He later represented Stoke-on-Trent from 1865 to 1868 and served
2 CONC  as the Member for Cambridge University from 1868 until his death in 1887. In addition to his parliamentary duties, he was a prolific writer on architectural and ecclesiastical subjects, and he was the founder of The Saturday Revi
2 CONC ew in 1855. He served as the President of the Royal Institute of British Architects and was appointed a Privy Counsellor in 1880. His public life was characterized by his advocacy for Gothic architecture and his involvement in var
2 CONC ious charitable and educational initiatives, including the restoration of St Augustine's Abbey.
2 CONT
2 CONT Sir Alexander Beresford Hope died on October 20, 1887, at his residence, Bedgebury Park, in Goudhurst, Kent, at the age of 67. His death was the result of a period of declining health, and he was buried on October 26, 1887, at Chr
2 CONC ist Church, Kilndown, in Kent, alongside his wife. His grave bears an inscription detailing his birth, marriage, and death dates, as well as those of Lady Mildred. His estate and genealogical records are preserved within the broad
2 CONC er context of the Hope and Gascoyne-Cecil family histories. He was succeeded in his estates by his eldest son, Philip, and his legacy continues through his contributions to British political literature and the architectural preser
2 CONC vation of mid-Victorian England.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
