1 NOTE in Dutch, Willem Frederik.  He was also grand duke of Luxembourg 
2 CONT (1815-40). In the War of the First Coalition against revolutionary France, 
2 CONT William commanded the Dutch army from 1793 until 1795, when his country 
2 CONT was overrun by the French. He thereafter lived in England and Germany, 
2 CONT returning to the Netherlands in 1813. The Treaty of Paris (1814) provided 
2 CONT that the Netherlands and Belgium be united into a single kingdom, and 
2 CONT William was proclaimed its first king. In 1815, by decision of the 
2 CONT Congress of Vienna, he ceded his hereditary properties in Germany to 
2 CONT Prussia, receiving in exchange the Grand Duchy of Luxembourg. William soon 
2 CONT estranged his Belgian subjects by his discriminatory policies, and he was 
2 CONT unable to prevent the secession of Belgium in 1830. He abdicated in favor 
2 CONT of his son, William II, in 1840 after repeated demands by his Dutch 
2 CONT subjects for a liberalized constitution. Despite his reactionary policies, 
2 CONT William greatly encouraged the agricultural and industrial development of 
2 CONT the Netherlands.
