1 OCCU
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} The history of the de Hooghes of Tielt goes back to 7 July 1500, when Willem de Hooghe and his wife Thaenkin (Tanneken) bought citizenship and moved into a house in Kortrijkstraat. .... Willem and Thaenkin had a so
2 CONC n, Olivier, . A man of substance, he served as magistrate, and from 1562 to 1564 as burgomaster. In that role, he was responsible for the suppression of heresy, an extremely troublesome issue in the years preceding the outbreak of
2 CONC  the Dutch Revolt.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Revolt took a heavy toll on the little town, which was first subjected to occupation by Calvinist troops from Ghent and then by equally ruthless Spanish mercenaries. With commerce and industry grinding to a halt, Tielt became 
2 CONC a ghost city. In 1593, the cloth-makers’ hall and the aldermen’s house collapsed; in 1603, plague ravished the town. Only the proclamation in 1609 of a Twelve Years’ Truce between Spain and the fledgling Dutch Republic heralded a 
2 CONC modest revival. During these troubled times, Olivier de Hooghe continued to serve his hometown in various official capacities.35 Around 1567, his son Jan de Hooghe married a woman from a prominent local family named Paschina or Pa
2 CONC squijntje van Ryckeghem. The artist Romeyn thought his great-grandmother Pasquijntje van Ryckeghem had a brother named Romeyn who served as schepen in Ghent. We know of no alderman of that name in Ghent, but the Tielt city account
2 CONC s do mention a Romeyn van Ryckeghem, presumably Pasquijntje’s brother.
2 CONT
2 CONT Like his father, Jan initially served his hometown during the difficult years of Calvinist rule. But unlike his father, he joined the increasing stream of refugees that left the sorrows of Flanders behind them. Settling in London,
2 CONC  he was active as a merchant and became a member of the Dutch Protestant refugee church worshiping in Austin Friars, a former Augustine monastery. The consistory records depict him as a quick-tempered and quarrelsome man. He negle
2 CONC cted to pay for merchandise delivered, threatened his creditors with a knife, and failed to display the required repentance. In June 1593, Jan made his last will and testament, which was to have a critical impact on subsequent gen
2 CONC erations. While bequeathing all movable and immovable property that he had left behind in Flanders to be shared evenly between his two sons, he explicitly cut out his elder son Jan  (‘for some certaine good causes’) from his Londo
2 CONC n estate and bequeathed all of it to Romeyn. The reason for the unequal treatment was that the younger son had joined his father in London, where he and his wife shared a house in Cornhill Ward with his father and his stepmother J
2 CONC anneken ’s Hooghen, and took care of them in their old age. There is no evidence attesting to the presence of Jan junior  in London. He probably remained in the Low Countries, either in order to keep an eye on the family property,
2 CONC  or because he had decided to remain true to the Catholic faith (or both)
2 CONT
2 CONT The younger son Romeyn, great-uncle of the artist, was the progenitor of the prosperous branch of the de Hooghe family. He was known in London as a silk weaver and silk braid worker, but he must have been active in the silk trade 
2 CONC as well. With his second wife Judith Bischop, the daughter of another refugee from the Low Countries, he had eight children. The family resettled in Amsterdam after the conclusion of the Twelve Years’ Truce, in a house near the ne
2 CONC wly built Commodities’ Exchange
2 CONT
2 CONT Source
2 CONT
2 CONT Henk van Nierop. (2018). The Life of Romeyn De Hooghe 1645-1708 : Prints, Pamphlets, and Politics in the Dutch Golden Age. Amsterdam University Press.
