2 NOTE Hi Christine, your mother's description for the reason for the photo sounds most plausible.  I remember something like that being told to me by my mother as well now that you mention it. Sadly she passed away just a year ago so un
3 CONC less I wrote things down a lot of that history is gone now. My mother was the youngest daughter of François Daubenton.  Thanks for the reminder of my birthday don't feel a year older than when I was in my thirties and forties! Tha
3 CONC nks for checking with your mother, regards Tony
2 NOTE And Happy Birthday soon!
2 NOTE Hi Anthony! I showed this picture to my mother (daughter of Charles Daubanton). She remembers that she was also in Geneva then, together with her parents. Tante Tini was a nurse in Geneva and never wanted to travel; Tante Bep was 
3 CONC a French teacher and lived in The Hague, oom Frans was involved in work for the Red Cross (being a doctor). At the time the picture was taken, my grandparents were in The Hague on holidays (my grandfather worked as a Dutch diploma
3 CONC t) and Oom Frans was in The Hague as well for a meeting of the Red Cross. So (because Tante Tini did not want to travel) they all decided to travel down to Geneva. 
2 NOTE I will show the photo to my mother. She was named after Tante Tini and Tante Bep (Adelgunde Christine Elizabeth) and people call her Bep. Perhaps she knows.
2 NOTE Hi Christine, unfortunately I don't know if this was a special occasion or not. There are two different handwritings on the back of the photo one I recognize as my mothers with the names and the other with the date and location.  
3 CONC At a guess I would say it was maybe the first time in awhile that all of them were together and maybe the last time they were?
2 NOTE Hi Anthony, How special to see this photo!  Thanks for posting it. Do you know the history behind it? Was it taken for a special ocasion?
2 NOTE Great photo Tony, thanks so much for posting it!
