1 OCCU Farmer (Gruisput, Jacobsdal; Duraville Modderrivier; Farramere, Benoni)
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Oupa Wouter rose above adversity  and severe hardship and became a very affluent man.  He was the only person in Benoni (after the depression of 1933) who owned a car. He was a very kind hearted man and always trie
2 CONC d to provide for those in need.  My mother (Dollie) used to tell me how she sat in the dicky sear of the car as her father would deliver meat, milk, cream , eggs, vegetables,  fruit and groceries to at least 10 families in Benoni.
2 CONC   And he did not forget the sweets for the little ones!  On the farm, his workers had brick built homes and they received  ample food, meat, vegetables and mieliemeal.  They were also given parts of the farm they could cultivate f
2 CONC or themselves.
2 CONT
2 CONT Oupa Wouter was a very hard worker.  He worked 24/7.  Dollie said the John Deere tractors were lined up neatly, with a little red Massey Fergusson tractor standing at the end of the line!
2 CONT
2 CONT Oupa Wouter had 2 beautiful horses called Whiskey(Black) and Soda(white).  She used to ride with him on Soda to inspect the fences, when they were living at Duraville.  Dollie said she and aunty Dalene would dam up the water Oupa 
2 CONC Wouter was trying to get to the crops.  They would happily be playing in the mud.  He had a difficult time with his two daughters who constantly caused him more work!
2 CONT
2 CONT Oupa Wouter could shoot very well and often won the rifle competitions.  Dollie would always want him to win the dates and honey prizes and he always had to forgo the first prize to appease his eldest.
2 CONT
2 CONT One day, after hearing the story about Moses in the Bible, she and aunty Dalene decided to play out the story.  She would me Miriam and uncle Wouter whoo was a sturdy 1 year old, would be Moses.  They put uncle Wouter in a basket 
2 CONC and dragged him over to the farm dam.  They struggled up to the edge of the dam and battled to get the basket over the edge as it kept on catching on the edge.  Just as they nearly got the basket over the edge, a big strong arm an
2 CONC d hand appeared out of the blue, rescuing his then, only son from drowning.  
2 CONT
2 CONT Sometimes he did not know what to do with Dollie when he was working, as she was constantly sabotaging his work.  One day he strung her up in a hessian sack and tied the sack up on a wooden beam in his shed.  She fell asleep, afte
2 CONC r wetting the sack.  Oupa Wouter had forgotten about her hanging in the shed. When Ouma Jeanette started looking for her, they found her still hanging on the beam, with a black cobra lying on the beam above her!  Oupa Wouter immed
2 CONC iately shot the snake, to Ouma Jeanette's shock and horror!  She told him off severely for the incident.
2 CONT
2 CONT After his death, many people came to Ouma Mary to pay back the money he had helped them with, when they needed help. Oupa Wouter was a great man.
